/*
 * Thingy:53 - button and LED selection for the MFS_1 toggle tool.
 *
 * BUTTON - the default is the deliberate choice, recorded here.
 *
 * The enclosure exposes ONE pushbutton and it is button0 (P1.14), which the
 * board already aliases as sw0. Nordic's own Thingy:53 applications treat
 * button0 as the single user button.
 *
 * The devicetree also declares button1 (P1.13), and that one MUST NOT be used:
 * the board aliases it as mcuboot-button0, so holding it while the device
 * resets is the MCUboot DFU-entry gesture. A demo whose button sometimes drops
 * the device into the bootloader instead of sending a toggle is worse than no
 * demo. No override is needed - the tool reads sw0 - but the trap is worth
 * writing down.
 *
 * LED - overridden below.
 *
 * The Thingy:53 has a single RGB LED exposed as three GPIO channels:
 * led0 = red (P1.08), led1 = green (P1.06), led2 = blue (P1.07). The tool
 * lights one LED for the duration of the advertising burst, so on this board
 * the channel it picks IS the colour:
 *
 *   red   - the board's only LED showing red is what a demo audience reads as a
 *           fault, and this indication means the opposite: the burst is going
 *           out.
 *   blue  - aliased as mcuboot-led0, so MCUboot blinks it during DFU. Reusing
 *           it at run time makes the two states indistinguishable.
 *   green - unambiguously "transmitting", and claimed by nothing else. Chosen.
 *
 * The application stays board-agnostic and keeps reading led0; the alias is
 * remapped here so that led0 means green on this board. Every other target
 * keeps its own led0.
 *
 * The same three pins are also wired to pwm0 through the board's pwmleds node,
 * but CONFIG_PWM is not enabled by this tool, so the PWM driver never
 * initialises and never applies its pinctrl. The pins stay under GPIO control.
 */

/ {
    aliases {
        led0 = &green_led;
    };
};
